section.para:
		The feedforward tagger is a small neural network over word, suffix, and word shape
		features. Train a FeedforwardPOSModel with
		FeedforwardPOSTrainer, wrap it in a FeedforwardPOSTagger,
		and call tag. The hyperparameters are passed as a
		FeedforwardPOSTrainer.Settings record in the order shown below;
		Settings.defaults() supplies a usable starting point. Training is
		deterministic for a fixed seed on a given platform and JVM.
		FeedforwardPOSTaggerUsageTest asserts the behavior shown here.

// embeddingSize, hiddenSize, epochs, batchSize, learningRate,
// l2, dropout, wordCutoff, suffixCutoff, seed
FeedforwardPOSTrainer.Settings settings = new FeedforwardPOSTrainer.Settings(
    16, 32, 80, 32, 0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 1, 1, 17L);

FeedforwardPOSModel model = FeedforwardPOSTrainer.train(sampleStream, settings);
FeedforwardPOSTagger tagger = new FeedforwardPOSTagger(model);

String[] tags = tagger.tag(new String[] {"the", "cat", "barks"});

Pretrained word vectors are an opt-in: an overload of train takes a java.util.function.Function that is asked once per distinct lowercased training word and returns its vector, or null for a word without one. The vectors of the training words extend the input layer and are stored inside the model, so tagging needs no separate embedding source and a word without a stored vector scores that block as zeros. A further overload also takes a coverage lexicon, extra words whose vectors are stored besides the training words; the lexicon does not change training itself, it only widens tagging-time coverage to words never seen in training. Without these opt-ins the two-argument train call above behaves exactly as before. FeedforwardPretrainedVectorsTest asserts the behavior shown here.
// Return one vector per word the source knows, null for a word without one. Function<CharSequence, float[]> wordVectors = word -> lookupVector(word);
FeedforwardPOSModel vectored = FeedforwardPOSTrainer.train(sampleStream, settings, wordVectors);
// Additionally store vectors for words never seen in training: FeedforwardPOSModel withLexicon = FeedforwardPOSTrainer.train(sampleStream, settings, wordVectors, lexiconWords);

		How confident was the tagger? topKSequences returns the greedy
		tagging as a single scored Sequence: getProbs()
		holds the model's probability for each assigned tag, one per token and each
		in the interval (0, 1], and getScore() is the sum of the logs
		of those probabilities.
		FeedforwardPOSTaggerUsageTest asserts the behavior shown here.

Sequence[] sequences = tagger.topKSequences(new String[] {"the", "cat", "barks"});
Sequence best = sequences[0];

// One probability per token, each in (0, 1]:
double[] probs = best.getProbs();

		Save a trained model to a file and load it for tagging:

Path modelPath = Path.of("pos-feedforward.bin");
try (OutputStream out = Files.newOutputStream(modelPath)) {
    model.serialize(out);
}

FeedforwardPOSTagger reloaded = new FeedforwardPOSTagger(
    FeedforwardPOSModel.load(modelPath));

String[] tags = reloaded.tag(new String[] {"the", "cat", "barks"});
